[s0l0!-header][h1]sizeof[/h1][/s0l0!-header]

While the size allocated for varying data types depends on the architecture of the computer you use to run your programs, C++ does guarantee a minimum size for the basic data types:  [img id="2460" width="100%"]
The [b]sizeof [/b]operator can be used to get a variable or data type's size, in bytes.
[s0l0!-header][b]Syntax:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="cpp" ][b]sizeof [/b](data type)[/code]
The [b]sizeof [/b]operator determines and returns the size of either a type or a variable in bytes. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="cpp" codeId="228"]cout << "char: " << sizeof(char) << endl;
cout << "int: " << sizeof(int) << endl;
cout << "float: " << sizeof(float) << endl;
cout << "double: " << sizeof(double) << endl;
int var = 50;
cout << "var: " << sizeof(var) << endl;
[/code]
[note]Output values may vary, according to the computer and compiler used.[/note]

