[s0l0!-header][h1]Size of an Array[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The C++ [b]sizeof [/b]operator is also used to determine the size of an [b]array[/b].
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="cpp" codeId="229"]double myArr[10];
cout << [b]sizeof[/b](myArr) << endl; 
[/code]
On our machine, [b]double [/b]takes 8 bytes. The array stores 10 doubles, so the entire array occupies 80 (8*10) bytes in the memory. 

In addition, divide the total number of bytes in the array by the number of bytes in a single element to learn how many elements you have in the array.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="cpp" codeId="230"]int numbers[100];
cout << [b]sizeof[/b](numbers) / [b]sizeof[/b](numbers[0]);
[/code]
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]
